  "poe": {
    "budgetW": 740,
    "sourceUrl": "https://apps.juniper.net/hct/product/EX2300%20Multigigabit/hwspecs",
    "note": "740 W from a 1005 W fixed supply: the per-model hardware specs, the power-supply table and the datasheet all say 740 W, while the PoE User Guide Table 5 says 750 W for this model as well as for the 48P. 740 W is recorded (three documents, and 48 x 15.4 W = 739.2 W). 802.3af/at only - the 10G-capable multi-gigabit ports are still PoE+ ports, not 802.3bt."
  },
  "fabric": {
    "capacityMbps": 176000,
    "kind": "packet-switching capacity",
    "note": "Datasheet figure of 176 Gbps, from the datasheet's 48-port grouping. Juniper's per-model hardware specs give 132 Gbps unidirectional / 264 Gbps bidirectional for this model, more than the 176 Gbps the datasheet quotes - the second contradiction in this family, stated in the PR body. 130 Mpps is common to both."
  },
  "sourceUrl": "https://apps.juniper.net/hct/product/EX2300%20Multigigabit/hwspecs",
  "notes": "Port layout from the datasheet's Table 1: gigabit ports 0-15 and 32-47, the sixteen multi-gigabit ports 16-31 in the middle, and six uplink cages (the only EX2300 with six). Juniper's quick start lists those 16 ports as 100/1000/2500/5000/10000BASE-T; they are modelled at 2.5G with speedCaps up to 5G because engine/media.js marks 10GBASE-T as carrying no power and every one of these ports is a PSE port, so a 10G model speed would silently strip PoE from the port. 14.45 in / 36.7 cm deep, 6.5 kg, four fixed side-to-side fans, 45.8 dB."
}
